::: {.doc-subject}

Check if the graph has an Eulerian path but not an Eulerian circuit.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L75083-L75153){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_semieulerian()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is semi-Eulerian](../assets/graphs/is-semieulerian.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: a bowtie plus an edge between B and E; badges give each node's degree and exactly two nodes, B and E, are odd (outlined), so a trail from one to the other uses every edge once and is_semieulerian() returns True. Right: the same graph plus an edge between A and D, which leaves four odd nodes (shown in the accent color), so is_semieulerian() returns False." width="100%"}

An Eulerian path is a trail that visits every edge exactly once. A graph is semi-Eulerian if
it has an Eulerian path but NOT an Eulerian circuit.

For undirected graphs, this occurs when:

- the graph is connected
- exactly two vertices have odd degree (start and end of path)

For directed graphs, this occurs when:

- the graph is weakly connected
- exactly one vertex has out-degree - in-degree = 1 (start)
- exactly one vertex has in-degree - out-degree = 1 (end)
- all other vertices have equal in-degree and out-degree
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph has an Eulerian path but not circuit, `False` otherwise.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_eulerian`) 

:   Check for an Eulerian circuit.

[](`~eulerian_path`) 

:   The path itself.

[](`~has_eulerian_path`) 

:   Check for an Eulerian path, open or closed.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A path can be walked end to end crossing each edge once, but it can't return to its start,
so it is semi-Eulerian. A cycle has a closed walk, so it isn't:

::: {#24cdc0fb .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

path = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C")])
cycle = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A")])

path.is_semieulerian(), cycle.is_semieulerian()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### One bridge fewer

`konigsberg()` is the city of Königsberg as Euler studied it in 1736, with seven bridges
joining its two islands and two river banks. All four land masses have an odd number of
bridges. Closing the Honigbrücke, between the two islands, makes both islands even and
leaves just the two banks odd, so a walk can cross each of the six bridges left exactly
once, from one bank to the other:

::: {#98164805 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
konigsberg = ec.konigsberg()
closed = ec.konigsberg().delete_edge("Kneiphof", "Lomse")

konigsberg.is_semieulerian(), closed.is_semieulerian()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(False, True)
```
:::
:::


